      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Think about a real sample of chlorine. It's a mixture of both isotopes, and they're not equally common. Out of every hundred chlorine atoms, about seventy-six are chlorine-thirty-five, and only about twenty-four are chlorine-thirty-seven. So to find the average mass of one atom, add up the masses of all hundred, then share the total among them. Seventy-six atoms at thirty-five, plus twenty-four atoms at thirty-seven, gives three thousand five hundred and forty-eight. Divide by a hundred atoms, and that's thirty-five point four eight. It's much closer to thirty-five, because most of the atoms are chlorine-thirty-five.",
