      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "That's a weighted average. Each isotope's mass counts as many times as that isotope occurs, so the common isotope counts more. Relative atomic mass, written A r, is this weighted average for the atoms in a natural sample of an element, on the scale where carbon-twelve is exactly twelve. Like any relative mass, it has no units. The percentages, called abundances, are measured with an instrument called a mass spectrometer, which sorts atoms by mass. How it works is a story for later. Here, the abundances are simply given.",
